The [[Popular Action (Peru)|Popular Action]], the largest party in the previous legislature, lost some of its seats, and previous parliamentary parties like [[Union for Peru]] (UPP) and the [[Broad Front (Peru)|Broad Front]] (FA) had their worst results ever, attaining no representation.<ref name="resultsfinch">{{cite web|url=https://www.fitchratings.com/research/sovereigns/elections-show-fissures-in-perus-political-institutions-14-04-2021|title=Elections Show Fissures in Peru's Political Institutions|publisher=Finch Ratings|date=14 April 2021|access-date=7 June 2021|archive-date=7 June 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210607090449/https://www.fitchratings.com/research/sovereigns/elections-show-fissures-in-perus-political-institutions-14-04-2021|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Peruvian Nationalist Party]] of former President [[Ollanta Humala]] and [[National Victory]] of [[George Forsyth (footballer)|George Forsyth]] (who led polling for the presidential election earlier in the year) failed to win seats as well.<ref name="resultsfinch" /> New or previously minor parties such as [[Free Peru]], [[Avanza País (Peru, 2017)|Avanza País]] and [[Together for Peru]] and [[Popular Renewal]], the successor of [[National Solidarity (Peru)|National Solidarity]], had good results, with Free Peru becoming the largest party in Congress.<ref name="resultsfinch" /> [[Contigo (political party)|Contigo]], the successor to former president [[Pedro Pablo Kuczynski]]'s [[Peruvians for Change]] party, failed to win a seat once again and received less than 1% of the vote.<ref name="resultsfinch" /> On 26 July, two days before Castillo was sworn in as Peru's President, an opposition alliance led by Popular Action member [[Maricarmen Alva|María del Carmen Alva]] successfully negotiated an agreement to gain control of Peru's Congress.<ref name=oppositionrule /> 
